  "state": {
    "case_id": "53",
    "case_summary": "A 42-year-old man underwent transthoracic echocardiography as part of a routine medical checkup, which revealed a left ventricular mass. He had no prior medical history and denied fever, weight loss, dyspnea, or other symptoms. On admission, his temperature was 36.5 °C, blood pressure 120/80 mm Hg, and electrocardiogram showed normal sinus rhythm. A chest radiograph revealed no active lung lesions. Transthoracic echocardiography demonstrated a mobile, smooth, oval, pedunculated mass originating from the left ventricular lateral wall, measuring 1.34 × 1.9 cm. Contrast-enhanced chest computed tomography showed the mass attached to the interventricular septum with focal contrast enhancement.",
